# CASP15 T1158v2 — Same Pump, Different Cargo (8SXB)
---

Remember MRP4, the prostaglandin pump?

This is the same transporter, but now caught
with a different passenger — prostaglandin E2.

PGE2 is the main mediator of pain and fever.
When you take ibuprofen, you're blocking
PGE2 production.

> close
> load 8sxb
---

The CASP15 organizers were clever here — they gave
predictors four different ligands bound to the
same protein.

Each ligand binds slightly differently,
and predicting those subtle differences
was the real challenge.

> spin 0.15
---

Here's PGE2 in the binding pocket.

Compare this to PGE1 — the pocket reshapes
slightly to accommodate each prostaglandin.
This plasticity is what makes docking
prediction so difficult.

> stop
> view ligand
---

This is why CASP matters — it forces the field
to prove their methods work on real,
unseen structures.

No cherry-picking, no peeking at the answer.

> reset
> spin 0.1
---

MRP4 with prostaglandin E2 — the pain molecule
in transit.
PDB code 8SXB, CASP15 target T1158v2.

> stop
